Let me map my rules to this problem:

1. Reckon before battle — five matters, seven comparisons, ordered chain 度→量→數→稱→勝. This is the core: the health check IS a temple reckoning. Five matters/seven comparisons for a harness. Ordered chain: ground gives measurement, measurement gives quantity, quantity gives number, number gives weighing, weighing gives victory. This maps: measure first (probe), then quantify, then weigh (compare), then judge. Very strong fit for a readiness check. Also 多算勝，少算不勝 — more reckoning wins.

2. Make yourself unbeatable first — the harness health is exactly "先為不可勝" — make yourself unbeatable before dispatching. Readiness = making oneself unbeatable. "Do not rely on the enemy not coming; rely on having the means to meet them."

3. Whole over broken; strike plans first, walled cities last — rank targets: cheapest/most-leveraged interventions first (plans > alliances > armies > cities). For build order and help: prefer the whole (don't break the harness config) — advice over auto-fix, and rank fixes by cost. Also "attacking walled cities is the lowest, the method when there is no alternative" — auto-editing config is the siege.

4. Know whether you can fight; know the other side and yourself — know the harness and yourself. "Knowing our troops can attack but not the enemy..." half-victory: a check that proves the tool exists but not that the permission is configured is half. Five ways to know victory. Also "不知彼而知己，一勝一負" — only self-knowledge is one win one loss.

5. Move only when it accords with advantage; never from anger — do not add checks without advantage; cost/benefit of each check. Don't build checks out of enthusiasm. Reversibility.

6. In deliberating, mix harm into benefit — each proposed check must state its harm (cost, false positive, upkeep) before its benefit. This is why the design must list the price of each check and why "help" must not over-reach.

7. War is the way of deception — maybe less applicable; could apply to prompt-injection defenses / not trusting config self-reports. "Act on the enemy's condition." Hmm, the harness config files "claim" things (reported status) — deception means don't trust the surface. Actually the statuses reported vs verified map well: a local file claims it (reported). My #7: war is deception; "when able, appear unable." The harness might look configured. Also #17 probing.

8. Value victory, not duration; speed — build order: get a working minimal check fast rather than a comprehensive slow one; "兵聞拙速，未睹巧之久也." Also supply from the enemy — reuse existing probes rather than building new infrastructure. This is a strong build-order rule: ship the small version, don't build the perfect broad probe set.

9. Bring the other side to you; do not be brought — the check should adapt the design to the harness, not the reverse? Or: make the health check set the terms. Hmm. Could apply: don't let the harness's default config dictate; make readiness bring the conditions.

10. Avoid the solid, strike the empty; have no constant form — probe where verification is cheap and certain, avoid the solid/contested/unknown config formats (U6). "水因地而制行" — shape by the ground: per-harness probes should be verified per installed version; where unknown, mark unknown.

11. Foreknowledge must come from people; pay for it — don't infer config state from analogy or from measurement alone ("不可取於鬼神，不可象於事，不可驗於度，必取於人"). For U6: the config format cannot be inferred by analogy from another harness; it must be taken from someone who knows — i.e., verified against the installed version (or a person who read it). Also: pay for the knowledge — running the verification is the cost. And treating the converted spy generously — maybe the "help"? Hmm. Actually this is powerful for U6: don't generalize key names from one harness to another ("不可象於事" — cannot be got by analogy with events); don't claim measurement without ground truth. Also 日費千金 — the cost of not knowing.

12. Seek it in 勢, made of regular and irregular; not from the people — change conditions not people. For "help": the help should change the harness condition, not exhort the person. Also don't demand reliability from agents when the config is the problem. "求之於勢，不責於人." Strong: when a condition isn't good enough, fix the ground (config/harness) rather than blame the operator. Also 奇正 — regular and irregular combinations: regular = deterministic probe, irregular = the sabotage/failure test. Hmm, maybe.

13. Not every road is taken, and not every command accepted — there are checks not to be taken. Don't check everything; skip requirements that are per-run (U7). Ruler's commands not to be accepted: the method's existing gates may claim coverage; verify (G6). Also "進不求名，退不避罪，唯民是保" — the design should protect the product, not the designer's agenda.

14. Choose the action by the ratio of forces — 十則圍之，五則攻之... Match the check's intrusiveness to the confidence/verification level. E.g., only hard-fail where verification is at strength 10-to-1; where weak, report. "小敵之堅，大敵之擒也" — a stubborn small check (false-failing on weak evidence) is captured. Numbers not valued for their own sake: don't add checks just for count; "兵非貴益多也." Strong for check design: don't add a check per requirement just to have ten; add only where force is sufficient. Also "無慮而易敵者，必擒於人" — the check must size up the harness version.

15. The general's faults: five dangers, six defeats — the health check itself has faults. The build/design must examine its own dangers: over-zealous (必死), over-cautious (必生), quick to fail (忿速), purity/curliness (廉潔 → shame = refusing any reported status), loving the people (愛民 → too many warnings, "可煩"). Six defeats: a check with a weak probe and too many targets = disorder. The reviewer must examine the checker's faults. Strong.

16. Orders clear first, then enforced; care that cannot command is useless — the output must be clear before it's enforced; don't fail a condition whose rule wasn't clear. "約束不明，申令不熟，將之罪也" — if the check's criterion isn't stated clearly, that's the check's fault, not the harness's. And "厚而不能使，愛而不能令" — a check that only shows warmth (all-green) but can't fail is useless. This directly supports the house rule "every check must be able to fail."

17. Learn the enemy by probing and by signs — 策之/作之/形之/角之: four ways to learn. This is the probe design algorithm: reckon (策), stir (作), make it show form (形), test in contact (角). For harness health: run the version probe (策), trigger a behavior and observe (作), read the config that reveals its ground of life/death (形), and actually exercise the risky path in a sandbox (角). Read signs: "辭卑而益備者，進也" — a config that looks minimal but has carefully set permissions... Actually more directly: the four probing methods are the four probe kinds, from cheap/indirect to direct contact. Strike the empty (#10): probe where verification is cheap. This gives the check taxonomy.

